WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.160
Scot Osterweil: turn the floor
over to Eric Klopfer who's going

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.600
to introduce today's speaker.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.080
Eric Klopfer: Thanks, Scot. Um,
so I will briefly introduce

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.650
Justin, who's gonna be talking
today about "Failure to

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.990
Disrupt", his new book on
educational technology and its

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.220
failure to disrupt educational
practices. And Justin is a

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.610
professor here in Comparative
Media Studies and Writing. And

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:00.690
before that, he was a researcher
and lecturer here at MIT. So

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.390
he's been associated with the
community for for many years I'm

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:08.880
doing he taught my my education
courses previously, and and ran

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.190
our, our partnership with the
Woodrow Wilson Academy for a

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:15.300
number of years, Justin has been
doing work that I think I would

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.510
describe as sort of healthy
skepticism about educational

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:23.100
technologies, for I think, most
of his career, including his his

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.180
doctoral work and a lot of his
work more recently. And, you

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.550
know, it's it's sort of taking
this lens on educational

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.180
technology that's both sort of
hopeful and, and critical in

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.600
terms of looking at the ways
that it ultimately influences

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.500
the practices of educators, as
well as the lives of the many

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.460
students involved in the
educational systems. Justin runs

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.940
our Teaching Systems Lab here,
which looks at a lot of issues

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.730
in professional development of
teachers use implementation of

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.690
educational technologies, equity
in education, and innovative

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:59.430
practices for for training, both
current and next generations of

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:05.670
teachers. So with that, I will
hand it over to Justin, and

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.820
thanks for coming to colloquium
today, Justin.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.650
Justin Reich: Thanks, Eric.

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:17.430
We have some great instructors,
writing instructors, who help us

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:22.740
teach the media studies classes
to undergraduates at MIT. And

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.940
they try to help Media Studies
undergraduates think about what

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.410
argumentation and Media Studies
looks like. And one of the ways

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:35.850
they describe it is to say that
there are producers of media,

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:41.460
and audiences of media and they
sort of interact in some way

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:45.660
through media in some kind of
broader social context. And

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.440
everything can get much more
complex than that. The producers

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.480
can become consumers, the
consumers, producers, all that

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.240
kind of stuff, but there, but
there's a kind of fundamental

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.890
structure to a lot of common
arguments. And I would say, a

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:03.750
lot of my work is interested in
that model as well. That there

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.220
are people who take and
facilitate media as educators,

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.400
and there are people who consume
that media as learners. And

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.700
sometimes they pass it back and
forth. In some kind of broader

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.240
context. You know, the thing
which distinguishes me as a

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.930
learning scientist from other
media studies scholars, is I

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:27.900
tend to be keenly interested in
how does that interaction change

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.830
human development? How does the
learner develop new capacities?

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.110
How are they able to do
different kinds of things than

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:38.340
they were before on the basis of
that interaction? And I'm hoping

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.290
that one of the things that can
come out of this conversation

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:44.490
is, is I'll present to you some
of the ways that I see the field

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.710
of education technology as a
learning scientist, and I hope

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.350
that folks will will ask
questions from a media studies

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.990
perspective and and make you
know, for the graduate students

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.750
make connections to the readings
and other kinds of things.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:58.650
You're working on faculty, the
research and other practices

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.650
that you see are germane. And
we'll see if we can make some

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:08.790
some connections that way. So
one place that I start my work

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.810
is that for the last 20 years,
people have made some really

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:18.030
extraordinary claims about how
new media might transform the

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:21.810
education landscape. In 2009, a
Harvard Business School

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.590
Professor Clay Christensen
predicted that by 2019, last

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:28.590
year, half of all secondary
school courses in the United

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.560
States would be online or
blended, that they would cost a

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:34.530
third as much to provide and
they would produce better

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:40.410
learning outcomes. In 2012, when
Massive Open Online Courses

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.800
exploded in higher education,
edX and Coursera Udacity

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.160
Sebastian Thrun, the founder of
Udacity, said 50 years there's

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.190
only 10 institutions of higher
education left in the world.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.970
They're going to be you know, a
concentrated set of mega

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.850
universities providing the
world's best learning content

00:04:56.850 --> 00:05:01.440
all around the world and Udacity
might be one of them. Sal Khan

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:05.730
in 2011, Ted Talks said, Let's
use video to reinvent education,

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.360
let's have individual students
sit down in front of computers,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.480
let's have a personalized
learning pathway around

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.390
mathematics for them that sort
of optimized for their

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:18.060
individual learning rate. And
we'll still have students and

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.130
teachers and things like that.
But they mostly get together

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:23.880
like to do interesting projects
and to, you know, reflect on

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.100
what they're learning and build
community, but the sort of heart

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.610
of skills development will
happen through these machines.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:36.360
And then 2013, Sugata, Mitra won
the TED Prize for his proposal

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.270
that we didn't even need schools
or educational institutions

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:44.490
anymore, that we could simply
give kids laptops and broadband

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:48.300
connection. And without any
institutional support, they

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:53.250
could learn anything by
themselves. And then earlier

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:57.240
this year, the world was
blighted by a global pandemic

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:02.160
and 1.6 billion learners were
sent home. And to some extent,

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:05.340
you might think that like, this
was the moment that education

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.210
technology was poised for, I
mean, prior to the pandemic, the

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.610
case that education
technologists had to make was

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.610
that they had a set of offerings
that would be better than the

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.640
existing traditional educational
system. But they didn't even

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.630
have to make that claim anymore.
They simply had to claim that

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.200
they could offer better learning
experiences, then, you know,

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.790
pandemic hobbled system in the
middle of an emergency pivot to

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:30.720
remote teaching.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.960
And I think during the last 20
years, one of the reasons why

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.360
the arguments of education
technologists, for the

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.860
transformative potential of
Learning Media was so powerful

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.640
is that we saw these
transformations happen in other

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:54.060
sectors. Journalism has been
profoundly reshaped by media,

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:58.740
government and civic media is in
the midst of a transformation.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:03.510
The word friend means something
different than when I was

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.730
growing up dating our
relationships. I mean, some of

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:09.660
our most intimate experiences
are mediated by technology, by

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:14.610
social media in particular, in
new ways. Doesn't it stand to

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.540
reason that the same kind of
thing shouldn't happen in

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.750
education as well? Like, why
would education be a sector

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.560
that's different from any other
sector? But I think most of you

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:28.230
know what has happened during
the pandemic, which is that, you

00:07:28.230 --> 00:07:32.880
know, education technology does
not right in on, you know, a

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:37.320
flaming horse to a winged horse
to save us all. In fact, I think

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.690
most people, most families,
especially younger children, but

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.090
I think many folks in higher
education and well have have

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:49.170
experienced something ranging
from like, this is adequate to

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.550
wow, this is really a disaster
for us and for our families. And

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:56.880
in fact, two of the technology,
I think the two most prominent

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:00.540
technologies of the pandemic are
two of the very oldest

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:04.980
technologies that we have. So so
I would argue that the two

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.440
technologies that have dominated
the pandemic are learning

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.790
management systems, which are
basically platforms that allow

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.790
people to pass documents back
and forth with one another thick

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:18.960
Canvas Schoology Google
Classroom. These were theorized

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:22.320
in the 60s and 70s. They were
commercialized in the 1990s,

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.220
they will made open source in
the 2000s. And then the other

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:29.490
you know, perhaps dominant
technology of the pandemic has

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.400
been what in the 1930s, when it
was introduced was called video

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:37.620
telephony now goes by the term
of video conferencing. And I

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:41.160
think what we saw both in higher
education and in K 12 education

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:47.190
was, you know, a series of
collective acts of conservatism,

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:51.330
small c conservatism, the likes
of which we will never again see

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:55.200
in our lifetime, faced with
dramatically changing

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.440
circumstances, you know, most of
the professor it like walked

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:01.680
away from their lecterns and sat
down in front of their home

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.790
office video cameras, and kept
teaching, you know, roughly the

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:09.690
same way that they were teaching
beforehand, despite all the

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:13.140
transformations happening in the
world, and despite the promises

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:16.980
of people that technology would
rearrange those relationships

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:22.620
even in normal times, let alone
pandemic times. So my task as a

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:28.980
writer in this book is to
explain why that is to explain

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.600
why these arguments of
transformation can be so

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:37.470
tractable at times can be so
compelling at times. But also

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.230
the argument is that they
shouldn't be so compelling

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:44.100
because they're routinely not
true, and that there are more

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:48.150
productive stances to take
towards the role of technology

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:57.120
in schools. Last year, before
the pandemic, Sal Khan gave an

00:09:57.120 --> 00:09:59.970
interview to a little trade
magazine called this

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.960
administration. So I'm sure
millions of people have seen Sal

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:07.080
Khan's TED Talks, I promise you
that it was like me and four

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:10.590
other people who read this
interview. In district

00:10:10.590 --> 00:10:12.780
administration magazine, it
turns out that in the last

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:16.410
decade, Sal Khan had not only
built Khan Academy, this library

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:20.400
of videos on instructional
topics, and an adaptive tutor

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.910
and other kinds of tools, but he
actually built a regular in

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.790
person school. As a private
school, I think it's in Silicon

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:30.840
Valley area, it costs 20 or
$25,000, a year to attend. And

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:33.810
his observation after working on
this project for a long time was

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:36.240
now that I run a school, I see
that some of this stuff is not

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:39.240
as easy to accomplish compared
to how it sounds theoretically.

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:45.930
And his new argument was that
actually a better way it was,

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:49.080
it's not going to be the case
that Khan Academy is going to

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:51.390
transform relationships in
schools, we are not going to

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:54.690
have students who are spending
all you know most of their time

00:10:54.690 --> 00:10:57.780
developing skills through these
individual pathways. And at the

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.270
end of their skill development
coming together for rich,

00:11:00.300 --> 00:11:03.930
intricate project based
learning. Instead, the model

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.720
that he's recommending is to
teach about the way you had been

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.960
teaching four days a week, and
then use Khan Academy his

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.890
practice problems one day a
week. And he says that's doable,

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:19.260
that's tractable. And it's also
has some benefits for students

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:23.580
math learning. Um, the thing
that struck me in a powerful way

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:28.020
about that argument was how
incredibly well established it

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:32.670
was. So you can go back to the
1990s. Here's an article from

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:36.960
1997, published by Ken Koedinger
at Carnegie Mellon University,

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:40.410
where they had done in the
Pittsburgh schools the exact

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:44.670
thing. They had built a series
of cognitive tutors, adaptive

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:49.110
tutors that responded to
students responses and gave them

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:52.290
progressively easier harder or
properly sequence math

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:55.770
challenges. And they told
teachers to use it three or four

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:57.840
days a week, I think they told
him to use it three days a week,

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.210
or they told him to use it one
to two days a week and teach in

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:03.120
a regular way, three or four
days a week. And they ended up

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:05.970
for the most part if they use it
using it one day a week. And

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.910
they found the same kinds of
things that Sal Khan found 25

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:12.120
years later, which is that if
you have people teach in a

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.580
regular way, four days a week,
and then use adaptive tutors as

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.520
practice problems one day a
week, it works a little better

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:21.930
than what had come before. You
know, another way to frame this

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.420
is that, you know, kind of
Academy I think, is raised like

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:28.080
100 and $50 million in
philanthropic support over the

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.890
last eight or nine years. And
you know, what they what they

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:34.440
learned from that investment,
you could have discovered with

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:35.670
it with a trip to the library.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:38.490
So,

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:47.120
when I contrast Sal Khan of 2011
with Sal Khan of 2019, I see two

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.030
different kinds of stances
towards education, technology at

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:53.750
work. And over the last two
decades. One of the most

00:12:53.750 --> 00:12:56.480
powerful stances has been the
charismatic stance, and I borrow

00:12:56.480 --> 00:13:01.070
this term from Morgan Ames, who
did a really lovely anthropology

00:13:01.310 --> 00:13:04.730
of the One Laptop per Child
program that was situated from

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:07.010
the media lab here at MIT. And
she wrote a book called the

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:11.420
charisma machine. And and talked
about charismatic technologists,

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:15.920
people who envision technology
as tools that can disrupt and

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.060
transform and rearrange existing
systems, and who imagined

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:23.270
futures that are brand new and
different because of new

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:27.170
technologies. And she contrast
that with actually sort of what

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:32.330
I see in Sal Khan in 2019. With
the tinkering stance that she

00:13:32.330 --> 00:13:35.330
draws from a book from from two
historians  David Tyack, and

00:13:35.330 --> 00:13:38.840
Larry Cuban called tinkering
towards utopia. And in the

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:42.650
tinkering stance, the assumption
is not that new technologies

00:13:42.650 --> 00:13:46.040
will disrupt and transform
educational systems, but rather

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:50.480
that these existing conservative
complex political systems will

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:53.810
domesticate new technologies,
they will take new technologies

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:56.870
and they will slot them into
particular niches for particular

00:13:56.870 --> 00:14:01.850
students, particular contexts,
particular subjects, and that

00:14:01.850 --> 00:14:06.020
the future in many ways can be
seen as an extension of trends

00:14:06.020 --> 00:14:10.430
from history that things change,
but they don't change

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:16.820
disruptively. They change
incrementally. They change step

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:21.830
by step. And in some ways the
the book failure to disrupt you

00:14:21.830 --> 00:14:26.300
know, is like a love letter to
the tinkers or, or a passionate

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:31.460
plea for honoring and respecting
the work of tinker's. And that

00:14:31.460 --> 00:14:34.910
is to say that that the
charismatic stance leads us to

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:37.700
sort of boom and bust hype
cycles around education

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:43.700
technology, in which we miss
allocate our resources. And by

00:14:43.700 --> 00:14:48.230
contrast, there are ways that
technology can help improve

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:52.730
existing systems for learning.
But they tend not to be

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:55.550
breakthroughs, like a lot of
things in human development,

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:58.730
they tend to be a few steps
forward and a couple steps back

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:03.860
and kind of maddeningly slowly
plodding. But perhaps,

00:15:03.890 --> 00:15:07.340
ultimately, you know, leading to
improvements, or potential

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:09.920
improvements in human capacity,
or at least, if we're going to

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.320
make investments in education
technology, that's the sort of

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:17.150
stance to bring to it. So the
first half of the book, sort of

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:21.590
reviews what I think is one of
the most useful disciplines for

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:25.700
the Tinker kind of personal
discipline, which is to start

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:29.720
from the assumption that any new
technology is situated in some

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:32.630
kind of history. And if we know
something about that history, we

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:34.760
can make some pretty good
guesses about how a new

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:39.680
technology will operate. So to
illustrate that principle, I

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:43.220
look at a set of technologies
that I call learning at scale,

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:47.180
learning environments for many,
many learners and few experts to

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:47.690
guide them.

00:15:49.130 --> 00:15:52.580
Typically, education technology,
evangelists have not promoted

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:56.510
calculators as transformative
tools of teaching and learning.

00:15:56.690 --> 00:16:00.800
Because there's not a sense that
calculator kind of on its own

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:04.640
provides a you know, a
personalized, individualized

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:08.330
curriculum that is scalable to
many millions of people. But

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:10.910
there are other technologies
we've created in which edtech

00:16:10.910 --> 00:16:14.690
evangelists have made that
promise. And they tend to fall

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:17.420
into three different categories.
And you can define those

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:20.480
categories by who guides the
sequence of learning activities.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:23.570
So there are learning
environments in which an

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:27.830
instructor selects the suggested
sequence of activities. And

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:32.420
those are things like Massive
Open Online Courses. There are

00:16:32.450 --> 00:16:35.930
algorithm guided large scale
learning environments like

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:39.830
adaptive tutors, where a
computing algorithm measures

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:43.100
student performance on some
dimension. And on the basis of

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:47.000
that measure, select some new
learning activity to suggest for

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:52.370
a student. And there are peer
driven, peer guided network

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:56.090
learning communities. You know,
at MIT, I use the example of the

00:16:56.090 --> 00:16:59.930
scratch programming language and
community where the learning

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:03.890
experience of scratchers is in
profound ways shaped by the

00:17:03.890 --> 00:17:07.520
community of peers that they
interact with. On the scratch

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:10.820
platform, almost all of us
participate in these kinds of

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:14.960
networks in some way, if you're
interested in how to do makeup

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:18.620
on your face in new ways, or how
to style your hair, or how to

00:17:18.620 --> 00:17:22.400
beat a level in a video game, or
how to do different kinds of

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:25.880
handicrafts. If you're watching
videos, posting pictures,

00:17:25.910 --> 00:17:28.910
reading Reddit threads, you're
probably participating in one of

00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:35.570
those kinds of networks. Each of
those three genres has a history

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:40.340
behind it. They tend to have
similar kinds of pedagogical

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:44.480
proclivities, they tend to use
similar, you know, to borrow the

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:47.660
terms of software developers,
sort of similar technology

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:51.140
stacks. So the first and the
other thing is that for most of

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:55.190
them, we have a kind of track
record of efficacy behind them.

00:17:55.760 --> 00:17:59.660
As the example of Ken
Koedinger's 1997 research and

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:04.310
Sal Khan's 2019 proclamations
point out that, you know, we

00:18:04.340 --> 00:18:07.640
human beings have been using
computers to try to teach other

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:11.510
human beings for as long as
we've had computers. This is not

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:18.380
a brand new field. This is a 60
year enterprise conducted with

00:18:18.410 --> 00:18:22.190
substantial funding by super
bright people at research labs

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:26.360
all over the world. And so if
you find a new piece of

00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:29.060
education technology, I argue
that you can sort of ask this

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:31.940
question who guides a sequence
of learning activities here, and

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:35.780
you can see how it sort of slots
into one of these three genres.

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:38.660
And if you know something about
the street three genres, you can

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:43.970
make a good guess about how some
new technology will shape the

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:47.300
future. I think I won't spend a
ton of time with this, although

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:50.450
we can come back to it. except
to say that, you know, for

00:18:50.450 --> 00:18:53.270
instance, I think that
instructor guided and algorithm

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:56.810
guided genres of learning
technology, they tend to be

00:18:56.810 --> 00:19:01.190
inspired by pedagogies that
emphasize direct instruction,

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:05.210
and experts communicating
information to novices, where by

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:08.720
contrast, peer guided learning
environments tend to be

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:13.100
interested in pedagogies of
apprenticeship, where you learn

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:16.010
not so much from the received
wisdom of experts, but through

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:19.490
the process of trying things
interacting with others sharing

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:23.870
your experience. In the in the
middle slot here, I indicate

00:19:23.870 --> 00:19:27.470
some technologies that I think
are quite common to these

00:19:27.470 --> 00:19:32.030
genres. So for instance, most
adaptive tutors, they really

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:35.750
have two main parts. They have
an auto grader, and the auto

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:38.990
grader is what allows us to
determine the level of human

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:42.830
performance so we can select
some other tasks. And almost all

00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:46.820
of them, no matter how fancy
they purport to be, no matter

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:51.950
how many billions of cells of
data they purport to collect, no

00:19:51.950 --> 00:19:55.400
matter how fancy you know, the
algorithms or the

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:59.330
parameterization they claim to
have, at their core, they almost

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:02.720
all use I'm variant of a
statistical toolkit called item

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:05.750
response theory. And item
response theory was developed by

00:20:05.750 --> 00:20:09.080
the Educational Testing Services
in the 1980s. And I'll describe

00:20:09.080 --> 00:20:11.870
it to you now. But I take maybe
three pages in the book to

00:20:11.870 --> 00:20:15.560
describe it to readers, with the
point being that that this is

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:20.540
not intractable. This is not
impossibly complex, which is

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:23.780
what education technology
evangelists often try to

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:26.990
convince us of, like, the
fundamental building blocks of

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:29.330
this are well known. And they're
usually old, you know, if you

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:33.020
see a sort of Ed Tech roadster
coming your way, like lift up

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:35.990
the hood, and you will see like,
Oh, that's a pretty old engine,

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:38.870
or that's a pretty old chassis,
or there's some well established

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:42.410
pieces here. And if we
understand those well

00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:45.110
established pieces, then we can
make some good guesses about how

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:48.230
a new technology will operate in
the future. Or we can identify

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:51.860
the pieces of a new technology
that are in fact, distinctly

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:55.310
new, like, it's very unlikely
that the whole thing that we've

00:20:55.310 --> 00:20:59.360
created is going to reimagine,
you know, pedagogies, that we've

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:04.250
been working on for thousands of
years in our society. But maybe

00:21:04.250 --> 00:21:06.470
they have some particularly
interesting tweak. And if you

00:21:06.470 --> 00:21:09.080
can identify that particularly
interesting tweak, then you can

00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:11.780
think of that as a place for
study and exploration and other

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:14.900
kinds of things. No, and I can
talk in more detail and do in

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:19.430
the book about, you know, how I
think math department head

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:22.640
should examine this history of
technology when making a

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:26.900
decision about how to implement
a new piece of math software in

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:30.620
their school, how a Vice Provost
for information technology might

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:33.290
do that, how a researcher might
identify what kinds of

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:35.600
interesting problems are out
there, how a designer might

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:38.180
think about how to approach the
development of new software.

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:42.660
So in the in the second half of
the book, that

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:47.010
what I try to tackle are,

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:49.920
is making clicking work

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:51.990
are

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:57.090
what I call for as yet
intractable dilemmas, for issues

00:21:57.090 --> 00:22:01.380
that come up time. And again,
across all of these different

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:08.370
genres of technology, problems
that if people interested in

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:12.990
technology at different levels,
funders, developers,

00:22:13.020 --> 00:22:16.890
researchers, implementers,
teachers, students, if we

00:22:16.890 --> 00:22:22.800
thought about these problems in
in creative ways, if we were

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:26.580
diligent about trying to address
them, that would be our best

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.880
chance of sort of tinkering our
way to success. And they and the

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:35.280
problems emerge from three key
features of educational systems.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.520
three key features of these
systems that I think are really

00:22:38.520 --> 00:22:42.780
central to understanding why
technology doesn't lead to

00:22:42.870 --> 00:22:47.280
disruptive society, shifting
change in education, in the way

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:50.940
it does in other fields. And the
first piece of the education

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:54.240
landscape is that education is
just immensely almost

00:22:54.270 --> 00:22:59.880
unfathomably complex. Somewhere
today, there was a teacher, it

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:02.610
was a seventh grade earth
science teacher who is starting

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:06.600
a new unit on plate tectonics.
And somewhere else, there was a

00:23:06.600 --> 00:23:10.710
kindergarten teacher who across
a zoom video conference was

00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:14.130
trying to explain to students
how to open a new tab in a

00:23:14.130 --> 00:23:18.450
browser or how to tie their
shoes. And somewhere else, there

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:24.060
was an advanced Mandarin class
being taught in a in a liberal

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:26.430
arts college somewhere else, and
you know, and then somewhere

00:23:26.430 --> 00:23:30.240
else, there's a two year degree
program where people are

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:34.710
learning how to be radiology
techs, the you know, and it's

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:37.230
not just that those subject
areas and those contexts are

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:40.650
different. But what you do the
interactions you have with your

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:44.190
students day to day, the the
content that you cover the

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:48.510
skills that you address, they're
constantly changing across the

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:53.130
180 days of a K 12 school year
or the 28 weeks of a two

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:57.480
semesters of higher education.
It's extremely difficult to

00:23:57.480 --> 00:24:01.470
think about how technologies
could be built to address all of

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:05.130
those kinds of diverse use
cases. And indeed, the

00:24:05.130 --> 00:24:09.930
technologies that we have, are
very uneven. They work well in

00:24:09.930 --> 00:24:13.800
some circumstances, but not
others for some people, but not

00:24:13.830 --> 00:24:18.540
others, in some contexts, in
some subjects, but not others.

00:24:19.140 --> 00:24:23.100
And I think that is one of the
you know, sort of fundamental

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:27.660
misunderstandings that people
often have of when they predict

00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:31.170
sweeping changes from education
technology. They're imagining

00:24:31.170 --> 00:24:33.210
that our technologies and I
haven't found the right metaphor

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:35.190
here, but they're like
bulldozers, or they're like

00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:38.190
Swiss Army knives, they sort of
clear everything out or they do

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:41.910
everything. But our technologies
are not that way. They solve

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:45.300
very particular problems.
Typically, they're like very

00:24:45.300 --> 00:24:49.470
specific head pegs. And the
complexity of education is just

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:53.100
this like manifold, huge
sweeping landscape of lots and

00:24:53.100 --> 00:24:57.300
lots of different kinds of
holes. And then our education

00:24:57.300 --> 00:25:00.000
system is especially here in the
United States, but certainly in

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:03.090
Lots of other places in the
world is shaped by profound

00:25:03.090 --> 00:25:07.950
inequality. We provision
students in their schools and

00:25:07.950 --> 00:25:11.520
students in their homes, very,
very different levels of

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:15.390
resources with which to approach
the challenge of education. And

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:18.630
that shapes every part of our
education system. And certainly

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:22.380
some of the some of the saddest
thinking and reflecting to be

00:25:22.380 --> 00:25:25.590
done about the pandemic, is how
it is both revealed and

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:31.140
exacerbated those inequalities.
So I'll briefly talk about these

00:25:31.140 --> 00:25:34.710
four dilemmas. And then maybe
I'll stop for a bit and and see

00:25:34.710 --> 00:25:37.230
what questions or thoughts that
folks have. But here, here are

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:39.390
four of the kinds of things that
I think whether you're

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:42.600
interested in network learning
environments, or learning games,

00:25:42.600 --> 00:25:45.090
or adaptive tutors, or MOOCs, I
think these are problems that

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:48.420
sort of cut across these
different kinds of approaches to

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:52.440
learning at scale. So one has to
do with complexity, and I call

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:57.180
it the curse of the familiar. If
you build a technology that is

00:25:57.180 --> 00:26:00.570
familiar to people, you can get
that technology adopted. The

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:03.930
most widely used technology,
education technology, maybe in

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:07.260
American schools, is a tool
called Quizlet, which was

00:26:07.290 --> 00:26:12.180
created by an MIT dropout and
and a former students of Eric's

00:26:12.180 --> 00:26:15.450
and the terrific guy. And
Quizlet lets you generate online

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:19.350
flashcards. And when you glance
at the operation of the Quizlet

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:21.600
website, you will
instantaneously understand

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:24.090
what's going on, you go Oh, this
is a flashcard. Like I'm gonna

00:26:24.090 --> 00:26:26.160
write question on one side and
answer on the other side, I'm

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:28.680
gonna test myself, I'm gonna
share the decks with other

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:30.030
people. These are flashcards

00:26:31.380 --> 00:26:35.310
because it is instantaneously
recognizable, it can spread

00:26:35.310 --> 00:26:39.450
very, very widely. But if we sat
down a bunch of experts in the

00:26:39.450 --> 00:26:41.460
American education system and
said to ourselves, like, what

00:26:41.460 --> 00:26:45.450
are the real problems that we
face here in education with

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:48.270
inequality? With the challenges
of the future of the labor

00:26:48.270 --> 00:26:50.790
market? What do we really need
to work on here? I think very

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:53.790
few experts would come up with
the answer like, man, we really

00:26:53.790 --> 00:26:56.190
have a dearth of flashcards out
there in the schools like we

00:26:56.190 --> 00:26:59.250
really have to improve flashcard
access for children across the

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:04.770
United States. The new
flashcards are neat, but they

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:08.550
because they don't offer any
kind of substantial change in

00:27:08.550 --> 00:27:10.740
the way that teachers and
students interact with one

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:14.520
another with the content, they
are very unlikely to lead to

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:17.820
substantial improvements. They
gain a certain number of

00:27:17.820 --> 00:27:20.160
inefficiencies, and they help
students memorize some things

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:24.120
better. But they're, they're not
sort of unlocking new pathways

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:27.240
of teaching and learning. By
contrast, we do build things

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:31.470
that unlock new pathways to
teaching and learning. Users

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:35.610
often find them confusing. So if
we create things, new

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:38.700
technologies that incorporate
alternative pedagogies, or

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:41.040
create new routines or
relationships between teachers

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:44.580
and students, it's often the
case that this confuses teachers

00:27:44.580 --> 00:27:47.550
and students. And these things
sometimes get passionately

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:51.720
adopted in small niches, but
very rarely spreading scale

00:27:51.750 --> 00:27:56.190
across systems. In the cases in
which we do have some of our

00:27:56.190 --> 00:28:00.120
most interesting technologies,
sort of get into schools and

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:02.310
start to spread and start to
change the way teaching and

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:06.510
learning happen, they tend to do
two things. And this is where in

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:09.300
the second half of the book I
tried to propose, you know, some

00:28:09.330 --> 00:28:12.930
so not sort of pat solutions.
But some approaches to

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:16.680
addressing these intractable
dilemmas. They tend to be able

00:28:16.680 --> 00:28:20.790
to be used for familiar ways
initially, and then span out

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:25.050
into new kinds of opportunities.
So Quizlet gets in in a very

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:27.930
simple way with flashcards, but
doesn't really take you anywhere

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:31.230
beyond flashcards. I have
colleagues who built this

00:28:31.230 --> 00:28:35.760
graphing calculator tool called
Desmos, which is at first glance

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:40.170
just does everything a ti 84
calculator does on your computer

00:28:40.170 --> 00:28:45.210
for free. But then beyond that,
there's a whole set of ways of

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:48.780
using this graphing software
that enables a whole different

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:53.010
kind of approach to teaching and
learning and mathematics. And so

00:28:53.010 --> 00:28:55.560
it meets people in a familiar
place and takes them somewhere

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.680
else. And then the second thing
that people do when they

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:01.440
successfully navigate the curse
of the familiar, and this is

00:29:01.440 --> 00:29:05.160
sort of a theme that cuts across
both the book failure to

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:08.160
disrupt. And a lot of my current
critiques of how we're

00:29:08.160 --> 00:29:12.330
addressing online learning
during the pandemic, is that the

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:15.000
people who are good at taking
technologies and spreading them

00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:18.660
and having new pedagogy spread
with them. They don't assume

00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:22.440
that new ideas will travel with
the technology. They assume that

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:26.700
they have to engage communities
of faculty members, teachers and

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:30.210
learners in pedagogical
exploration about how to do new

00:29:30.210 --> 00:29:35.370
things. So the you know, MIT
Media scratch lab, they built

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:38.460
scratch, but they are also in
the midst of building this giant

00:29:38.460 --> 00:29:42.180
apparatus to teach people all
over the world. What are the

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:46.440
pedagogies of computational
creativity that are associated

00:29:46.440 --> 00:29:49.320
with scratch because scratch by
itself, if you sort of drop it

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:53.640
into schools, it will be it will
be, you know, domesticated by

00:29:53.640 --> 00:29:56.850
those schools for conventional
teaching and learning. But it

00:29:56.850 --> 00:30:00.300
provides an opportunity for
allowing people to remember And

00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:04.380
those ideas, but only if faculty
and students and communities are

00:30:04.380 --> 00:30:07.530
supported in doing that learning
and doing that rethinking you

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:09.930
know, it's a, it's a way of
thinking about the scaling of

00:30:09.930 --> 00:30:13.140
new ideas, not through
technology distribution, but

00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:16.530
through movement making, through
community building through the

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:18.900
kinds of things that we see in
other forms of social movements.

00:30:20.460 --> 00:30:23.820
A second challenge I call the
edtech, Matthew effect. And

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:27.750
Matthew effects are commonly
observed across sociology, they,

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:31.260
you know, it comes from a line
in the book of Matthew, which is

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:35.790
paraphrasing, like, he has much,
much more given, and he who has

00:30:35.790 --> 00:30:40.350
little more will be taken away.
There is a persistent story in

00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:43.230
education technology that will
have the capacity that new

00:30:43.230 --> 00:30:46.800
technologies will democratize
education, that they will make

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:50.490
education more free, more fair,
more, just, my colleague, Larry

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:54.270
Cuban, who's an emeritus
professor at Stanford, has

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:58.080
marked how these arguments go
back to the days of radio is a

00:30:58.080 --> 00:31:01.890
great book called teachers and
machines, in which he has a

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:04.500
picture of a bunch of young
people sitting around a radio

00:31:04.500 --> 00:31:07.680
receiver, that's the size of a
small child, you know, one of

00:31:07.680 --> 00:31:10.080
the big stand up units, and the
caption is, you know, with

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:12.750
radio, the underprivileged
school becomes a privileged one.

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:17.670
And you all know that radio did
not, you know, squash the

00:31:17.670 --> 00:31:21.630
inequalities that occur between
our schools, the United States,

00:31:22.230 --> 00:31:26.850
and nor will other technologies
that we develop on their own new

00:31:26.850 --> 00:31:30.450
technologies disproportionately
benefit the athlete, because

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:33.090
those people have the financial
and social and technical capital

00:31:33.090 --> 00:31:36.960
to take advantage of new
innovations. And it's only

00:31:36.960 --> 00:31:40.830
through deliberate efforts at
really thinking about what would

00:31:40.830 --> 00:31:44.280
it look like to create
technologies that close gaps

00:31:44.310 --> 00:31:49.350
rather than spread us further
apart? through things like do

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:53.130
our technologies, you know, can
we measure and assess, or

00:31:53.130 --> 00:31:55.890
observe how people from
different backgrounds and life

00:31:55.890 --> 00:32:00.960
circumstances use technologies
differently? A tragic thing that

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:04.020
we're going to research over the
next year, and and learn in

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:07.410
different ways is that white
children in American schools and

00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.540
black children in American
schools are not going to be

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:14.130
treated the same way on zoom,
that the behaviors of the you

00:32:14.130 --> 00:32:16.860
know, the subjectively
inappropriate behaviors of black

00:32:16.860 --> 00:32:21.450
students on zoom calls will be
policed in ways that they are

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:26.190
not for white students. And if
we don't find ways of looking at

00:32:26.190 --> 00:32:28.980
what's happening during the
current pandemic, and looking at

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:31.230
it through the lens of how do
people from different

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:33.570
backgrounds and life
circumstances, experience

00:32:33.570 --> 00:32:37.980
technology differently, then we
will miss the opportunity to

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:40.830
learn about those things and
figure out how we might be able

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:45.900
to address them. A third
challenge relates to unevenness,

00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:49.410
which is the trap of routine
assessment, which is that many

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:53.040
of our large scale Learning
Technologies depend upon

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:56.820
automated assessment. And we
have some domains in which we do

00:32:56.820 --> 00:32:59.520
really good automated assessment
and some domains where we don't

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:04.140
if you ask someone a question
with a well defined right

00:33:04.140 --> 00:33:08.940
answer, computational question,
a question in a physics system

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:13.200
where the laws of physics are
well defined, we can build good

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:17.340
auto graders that evaluate those
responses from learners. One of

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:21.210
the fields in which we've built
the most impressive auto graders

00:33:21.210 --> 00:33:24.120
in the field of computer
programming, where computer

00:33:24.120 --> 00:33:26.910
science professors and teachers
can assign their students

00:33:26.910 --> 00:33:29.280
computer programs to write that
have to meet certain engineering

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:33.000
challenges. And they can create
computer programs that grade

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:37.860
those assignments. And when you
can do that in an automatic way,

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:41.190
you can build systems where
people at their own pace and

00:33:41.190 --> 00:33:44.640
time can participate learning
experiences, get some feedback

00:33:44.640 --> 00:33:48.480
from an automated system, be
motivated and inspired by their

00:33:48.480 --> 00:33:51.480
progress or be supported by
different kinds of feedback, and

00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:54.540
then proceed and move forward in
their learning. There are lots

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:58.140
of domains we can't do this very
well, probably the most

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:00.990
important one is that we really
don't have good tools for

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:04.650
evaluating writing, for
evaluating people's ability to

00:34:04.650 --> 00:34:07.950
reason from evidence. This is a
problem because much of what we

00:34:07.950 --> 00:34:10.500
teach in the liberal arts
education is how to reason for

00:34:10.500 --> 00:34:14.520
evidence. Perhaps most
problematically, the things that

00:34:14.520 --> 00:34:17.760
we're good at building computers
to assess tend to be the kinds

00:34:17.760 --> 00:34:20.580
of things that computers are
already good at. They're highly

00:34:20.580 --> 00:34:24.210
structured, highly routine kinds
of problems, that arguably, we

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:26.790
don't really need people to do
that much anymore.

00:34:28.200 --> 00:34:31.290
By contrast, the things where
humans have a competitive

00:34:31.290 --> 00:34:34.290
advantage over machines in the
labor market, or sort of

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:38.760
equivalent contact concept in
the Civic sphere, are areas in

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:41.370
which we don't have very good
automated assessment. So you

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:43.560
know, the problem here is that
we're we're really good at

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:45.990
creating assessment systems for
things that we don't need people

00:34:45.990 --> 00:34:50.100
to do anymore. And this is not
because there aren't smart

00:34:50.100 --> 00:34:54.180
people who are working on this
problem. decades of work by

00:34:54.180 --> 00:34:56.700
very, very smart people in
technology companies and

00:34:56.700 --> 00:34:59.820
universities have thrown all
kinds of resources at this

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:03.720
challenge. And instead of
having, you know, some kind of

00:35:03.720 --> 00:35:08.100
Moore's Law, like exponential
growth and improvement, we've

00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:12.330
seen very, very little progress
here in decades. And I think

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:16.260
that should give us some
humility and caution about about

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:19.290
our predictions for the future
for lots of education technology

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:22.230
evangelist, it will, we'll make
a prediction and then when

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:24.300
doesn't come true, they'll say
add just we just need a little

00:35:24.300 --> 00:35:30.480
longer. Thomas Edison in 1913,
said that by in 10 years, all

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:34.380
textbooks would be replaced by
the films that he was producing.

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:39.360
And then in 1923, he gave, I
think it was in front of the

00:35:39.360 --> 00:35:42.510
FCC. And he made a similar
argument, except he said, Well,

00:35:42.510 --> 00:35:45.810
actually, it's going to take 20
years, but it'll happen that all

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:49.530
the textbooks will be replaced
by filmstrips. And we still have

00:35:49.530 --> 00:35:53.460
not replaced, you know, 100
years later, all of the all of

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:55.980
the textbooks with video
materials, because it turns out

00:35:55.980 --> 00:35:58.680
that print is a pretty good
media for learning in a lot of

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:03.450
different ways. Then the last
challenge that I mapped out is

00:36:03.450 --> 00:36:06.990
that if you're someone who's
excited about building and

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:11.040
improving software platforms,
you're probably very interested

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:14.340
in the large amounts of data
that these systems can collect.

00:36:14.520 --> 00:36:18.360
And then the ability to rapidly
run experiments that let you

00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:21.840
test how changes the software
platform affect people's

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:27.030
experiences. You know, there are
all kinds of regulatory issues

00:36:27.060 --> 00:36:28.980
with this. And there's all kind
of, you know, sort of cultural

00:36:28.980 --> 00:36:32.460
policy issues. There's some
sense in society, that if I go

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:37.020
to Amazon, to buy a book, that
they should be able to collect

00:36:37.020 --> 00:36:40.860
some data about my experience
there. And that, you know, if I

00:36:40.860 --> 00:36:43.590
find out that sometimes they're
doing, you know, randomized

00:36:43.590 --> 00:36:45.900
control trials to see if I'm
more likely to buy a book with a

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:49.320
blue button, or a red Buy
button, that, you know, at least

00:36:49.320 --> 00:36:54.000
I as an individual entered into
that learning experience into

00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:57.810
that software experience,
somewhat of my own free will and

00:36:57.810 --> 00:37:01.380
volition, not entirely. But But
as wicked as the problem is in

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:05.850
retail, it's much, much worse in
education. Because you know,

00:37:05.850 --> 00:37:10.920
right now, all across the world,
young people are both compelled

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:14.100
to go to school. And they are
compelled to use the software

00:37:14.100 --> 00:37:17.670
platforms that are assigned by
their teachers and school

00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:21.660
systems. And so there are all
kinds of reasons to be very

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:25.140
seriously concerned, both about
the immediate risks of

00:37:25.140 --> 00:37:28.380
surveillance in these systems,
but also about the long term,

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:32.400
kind of educational social risks
of normalizing and socializing

00:37:32.400 --> 00:37:36.030
young kids to live in
surveillance cultures. There are

00:37:36.030 --> 00:37:38.760
lots of circumstances in the
social sciences where we think

00:37:38.760 --> 00:37:43.680
experiments are like generally
good, we tend to not intuitively

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:47.700
think of experimenting on young
children learning as a thing,

00:37:47.700 --> 00:37:50.760
which is good, I think, I think
there are ways to do these

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:55.920
things responsibly. But I also
think that there are real

00:37:55.920 --> 00:37:58.860
serious concerns that are that
are embedded in these practices.

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:01.950
And if we want to see the same
kind of improvements in learning

00:38:01.950 --> 00:38:04.620
technologies that we've seen in
other kinds of retail software

00:38:04.620 --> 00:38:08.160
platforms, we're gonna have to
resolve and negotiate some of

00:38:08.160 --> 00:38:09.330
these kinds of challenges.

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:14.550
So that's what the second half
of the book tries to address, it

00:38:14.550 --> 00:38:16.770
tries to chart out, you know,
the first half is really more of

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:19.320
a history, the second half is
more of a have an engineering

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:22.920
text of trying to say, Here are
four common problems that we run

00:38:22.920 --> 00:38:25.740
into all the time. And here's
some approaches that I think

00:38:26.010 --> 00:38:29.850
might be able to solve them.
And, um, you know, as I

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:34.680
reflected on, you know, what the
lessons of this, you know, in

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:37.650
the prologue, I mentioned that
the final copy edits of the book

00:38:37.650 --> 00:38:43.200
were done, like March 23, the
book was written right at the

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:47.640
end of one era, and just at the
beginning of this new one, and a

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:50.250
lot of what I've done over the
last eight months is both try to

00:38:50.250 --> 00:38:54.030
be helpful, but also try to
reflect on what do we learn from

00:38:54.030 --> 00:38:57.000
the pandemic. That's, that's,
that's salient from history. And

00:38:57.000 --> 00:39:02.220
I think two important lessons,
at least come through. One

00:39:02.250 --> 00:39:05.520
constant mistake that education
technologists make is to

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:09.030
describe technology as sweeping
to describe it as something that

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:13.380
can sort of sweep away existing
systems and usher in new ones.

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:16.950
And instead, for a variety of
reasons, our education systems

00:39:16.980 --> 00:39:20.760
are our conservative
institutions, because they're

00:39:20.760 --> 00:39:23.430
extraordinarily complex. And
they're managing all these

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:27.000
different, competing interests.
And as a result, new

00:39:27.000 --> 00:39:29.760
technologies tend to be
domesticated, we tend to slot

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:32.760
them into existing functions,
which is why we perhaps

00:39:32.760 --> 00:39:35.220
shouldn't be surprised that all
the faculty walked away from

00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:39.450
their lecterns and went to their
home webcams. And you can decry

00:39:39.450 --> 00:39:42.330
that as you know, a sort of
pitiful conservatism and a

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:47.670
system. Or you can say, Wow,
this system is so well honed to

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:51.210
meet its competing interests,
that it's actually kind of found

00:39:51.270 --> 00:39:54.720
a local maximum a local
optimization of all the

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:58.080
different resources and
competing constraints. And then

00:39:58.140 --> 00:40:01.050
the second problem that I think
technology Just often make,

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:04.950
which is quite salient to our
moment now is that technologists

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:07.740
often describe education
technology as a switch that you

00:40:07.740 --> 00:40:11.100
can flip on and on, you sort of
buy it, install it, and then it

00:40:11.100 --> 00:40:14.370
works. And that's not at all how
education technology works.

00:40:15.480 --> 00:40:20.280
Education technologies are only
as useful as their communities

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:25.560
of users are well supported and
strong. They are powerful tools

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:29.280
for rethinking, learning for
imagining iterative and

00:40:29.280 --> 00:40:33.840
continuous improvements to
learning. But there's very, very

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:36.750
few things that we've created
that just kind of instantly in

00:40:36.750 --> 00:40:40.230
meaningful ways benefit
learning. Rather, they become

00:40:40.230 --> 00:40:44.100
useful. And we're seeing now all
over the world, millions of

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:47.520
faculty members engaged in the
process of asking themselves,

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:51.630
okay, I'm forced to use this new
technology now, what are what am

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:54.240
I doing? What am I colleagues,
my discipline, or my subject or

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:58.800
my school doing? And how do I
have to rethink my practice or

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:05.010
rethink my approach to have more
powerful, more effective, more

00:41:05.010 --> 00:41:08.220
connecting more expired
inspiring experiences from

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:12.360
learning. So those those two
things of technology is

00:41:12.360 --> 00:41:16.170
particular and domesticated by
systems rather than sweeping.

00:41:16.770 --> 00:41:20.130
And the strength of technology
not being its instantaneous

00:41:20.160 --> 00:41:25.530
effects, but by its ability to
be absorbed by, by community of

00:41:25.530 --> 00:41:28.650
learners, are perhaps the two
things that I'll leave you with

00:41:28.650 --> 00:41:30.690
before, before taking some
questions.

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:47.430
Scot Osterweil: And I just want
to invite everybody. If you're

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:51.960
up, if you're currently a
panelist, I think it's fine to

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:55.740
just unmute yourself and call
out at this moment.

00:41:57.750 --> 00:41:58.410
Just be

00:41:59.970 --> 00:42:04.170
mindful of someone who speaks up
before you. If you are a guest,

00:42:04.170 --> 00:42:15.390
feel free to put a question in
the q&amp;a. So I'm going to start

00:42:15.390 --> 00:42:21.000
with a question then, because no
one else is calling out. I know,

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:23.640
in the beginning of my work in
educational technology, I

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:29.820
realized it was a fallacy. But I
had this sort of way, I guess it

00:42:29.820 --> 00:42:36.870
was a wish, that of a technology
in my case game sort of made,

00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:42.600
that it could somehow make
people who used it aware of

00:42:42.630 --> 00:42:47.520
whole different models of
pedagogy, and when in fact, what

00:42:47.520 --> 00:42:50.070
we must learn is that teachers
tend to even if they're using

00:42:50.070 --> 00:42:53.070
something as radical for them as
a game, they're going to tend to

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:58.170
fall back on the same pedagogy
they've always used. Are there

00:42:58.170 --> 00:43:00.840
exceptions that are is that as
Yeah, as Eric point yes,

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:04.350
mentioned the Trojan mouse that
was that was a term that he has

00:43:04.350 --> 00:43:09.120
used? Are there no, is there no
evidence of that? Is there some

00:43:09.120 --> 00:43:12.600
evidence of that of the
technology actually shifting the

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:14.580
thinking of the people who adopt
it?

00:43:15.140 --> 00:43:18.350
Justin Reich: I mean, my view is
that there's not good evidence

00:43:18.380 --> 00:43:20.900
of the technology, or maybe
three things, there's not good

00:43:20.900 --> 00:43:23.180
evidence of the technology in
and of itself, shifting people's

00:43:23.180 --> 00:43:29.540
views. A second thing that there
that there, there is good

00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:34.550
evidence of is that technology
has an ability to catalyze

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:41.780
thinking about those kinds of
issues. So it's possible to you

00:43:41.780 --> 00:43:46.460
know, you could you can go into
a group of faculty members and

00:43:46.460 --> 00:43:49.160
say, the future is going to be
really different. The world is

00:43:49.160 --> 00:43:51.440
changing, our learners have a
whole set of new experiences.

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:53.720
And as a result, we think you
should rethink your

00:43:54.110 --> 00:43:57.500
relationships, your curriculum,
your pedagogy, all those kinds

00:43:57.500 --> 00:44:02.030
of things. And many faculty
respond with like, No, thank

00:44:02.030 --> 00:44:05.360
you, we're doing fine. And then
you bring a learning game into

00:44:05.360 --> 00:44:08.300
that same community experience,
you know, or some other form of

00:44:08.300 --> 00:44:11.120
technology. And it, you know,
operates as kind of like a

00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:14.330
symbol of the future to be able
to say, oh, like, we could do

00:44:14.330 --> 00:44:18.980
things really differently here.
We could, you know, this gives

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:22.130
us new opportunities and new
affordances new ways to rethink

00:44:22.130 --> 00:44:25.400
things. And so I think it can
catalyze those conversations.

00:44:26.090 --> 00:44:29.870
And then another thing that we
know is if people do move from

00:44:29.900 --> 00:44:33.320
more familiar practices to new
practices, it's a developmental

00:44:33.320 --> 00:44:37.070
process. You know, Judith
Sandholtz did this project in

00:44:37.070 --> 00:44:41.090
the 1980s, called the apple
classrooms of tomorrow, where

00:44:41.090 --> 00:44:45.320
she, with a team of folks, you
know, got some k 12 classrooms

00:44:45.320 --> 00:44:48.860
and got a bunch of Apple two
plus computers and had wired

00:44:48.860 --> 00:44:52.100
network with, you know, some of
the first network computing

00:44:52.100 --> 00:44:54.620
environments and they had robots
and printers and things like

00:44:54.620 --> 00:44:58.490
that. And she described teachers
going along a developmental

00:44:58.490 --> 00:45:02.270
trajectory from you. Things in
more familiar ways to using

00:45:02.270 --> 00:45:05.270
things in slightly novel ways to
doing really imagine at work.

00:45:06.230 --> 00:45:09.290
Faculty members travel along
that continuum at different

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:09.860
rates.

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:13.320
to a limited extent, they start
in different places,

00:45:13.320 --> 00:45:14.400
but really a lot of

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:17.820
them start at that first spot,
like what is the moat? You know,

00:45:17.820 --> 00:45:22.260
how do I use this to extend
existing practices? And then I

00:45:22.260 --> 00:45:26.880
can think about new and
different and interesting kinds

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:32.220
of things. So I, you know, and
that's, and that's why I think

00:45:32.220 --> 00:45:38.010
it's so important to pair the
idea of technology integration

00:45:38.100 --> 00:45:41.130
with the idea of Community
Learning at the same time, like,

00:45:41.130 --> 00:45:44.550
Can games do that? Sure. But not
when games fall from the sky,

00:45:44.700 --> 00:45:49.320
it's more likely to happen when
games arrive with a community of

00:45:49.320 --> 00:45:51.390
people who are interested in
teaching and sharing these

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:54.450
ideas. I mean, Mitch Resnick has
a great line that he'd had it

00:45:54.450 --> 00:45:58.110
with the release of scratch 3.0,
which was something like, you

00:45:58.110 --> 00:46:01.290
know, we're gratified at how
